<h3>Winter Storm Uri, February 2021</h3>

<p>Uri was the storm that taught many San Marcos homeowners where their pipes run. The <a href="https://www.weather.gov/media/ewx/wxevents/ewx-20210218.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service summary</a> lists 7.0 inches of snow 5 miles west-northwest of San Marcos and 5.9 inches in Kyle on February 15, after days of freezing drizzle that iced roads and bridges. Extended cold, combined with widespread power outages across Texas, led to burst pipes throughout the region. FEMA's major disaster declaration, <a href="https://www.fema.gov/disaster/4586" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DR-4586</a>, included Hays County for both Individual and Public Assistance.</p>

<h3>The 2023 ice storm</h3>

<p>Two years later, freezing rain fell on and off from January 30 through February 2, 2023. Ice reached half an inch in Dripping Springs and more than a third of an inch in Wimberley and Driftwood. Ice loads like that can snap limbs and bring trees down onto roofs, fences, and power lines. The federal declaration for this storm, <a href="https://www.fema.gov/disaster/4705" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DR-4705</a>, covered Hays County for Public Assistance only, which helps local governments rather than individual property owners. For most homeowners, recovery ran through their own insurance. See our <a href="/storm-history/">San Marcos storm history</a> page for more past events, and our <a href="/service-areas/dripping-springs/">Dripping Springs</a> page if you are in the hardest-hit area.</p>

<h2>How insurance treats burst pipe claims in San Marcos</h2>

<p>Most freeze claims are water damage claims at heart. The question is usually not whether water caused the damage but whether the policy conditions were met. Here is how common situations tend to play out, subject always to your own policy wording.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>How many policies respond</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Occupied home, heat on, pipe freezes and bursts</td><td>Water damage typically covered as sudden and accidental discharge</td><td>Deductible; mold and water damage sublimits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Power outage stops the heat, pipe bursts</td><td>Often covered when the owner did not cause the loss of heat</td><td>Policy's freezing language and any power failure exclusion</td></tr>
<tr><td>Home vacant or unoccupied, heat off, water not shut off</td><td>Freezing damage often excluded</td><td>Definitions of "vacant" and "unoccupied"; the heat and shut-off conditions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost to repair the broken pipe itself</td><td>Often not covered</td><td>Tear-out and access coverage to reach the pipe</td></tr>
<tr><td>Slow leak from a pipe that cracked weeks earlier</td><td>Often excluded as continuous or repeated seepage</td><td>Timeline of when the leak started and was found</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fire sprinkler line freezes in a commercial building</td><td>Depends on protective safeguards and heat conditions</td><td>Commercial policy conditions and endorsements</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Sudden and accidental vs. failure to maintain heat</h3>

<p>Many homeowners policies cover water damage that results from a sudden and accidental discharge from a plumbing system, including one that freezes. Many also contain a freezing exclusion that applies while the dwelling is vacant, unoccupied, or under construction, unless you used reasonable care to maintain heat in the building, or shut off the water supply and drained the system and appliances. In plain terms: if no one was living there and nobody kept the heat on or drained the lines, the claim may be in trouble.</p>

<p>Disputes often turn on facts. Was the heat set to a reasonable temperature? Did the power fail, leaving the owner with no way to keep it on? Was the house "unoccupied" for a weekend or for months? Those details matter, so write them down while they are fresh, including thermostat settings and any utility outage notices.</p>

<h3>Vacancy and unoccupancy</h3>

<p>San Marcos has many rental homes, student housing near Texas State University, and properties between tenants or on the market. Policies often treat vacancy differently from short absences, and some limit or exclude certain losses once a building has been vacant for a set number of consecutive days. Landlord and commercial policies have their own conditions. If a property will sit empty during winter, read the vacancy clause and ask your agent before the cold arrives.</p>

