<h2>Your mitigation duties after a pipe bursts</h2>

<p>Nearly every policy requires you to take reasonable steps to protect the property from further damage. Water keeps causing harm for as long as it sits, and mold can start within days. These steps protect both the house and the claim:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Shut off the main water valve</strong> and, if water is near outlets or fixtures, the electricity to affected areas.</li>
<li><strong>Document before you clean.</strong> Take photos and video of the burst pipe, the water, and every affected room.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the broken section of pipe.</strong> It shows how the failure happened.</li>
<li><strong>Start drying.</strong> Extract standing water, run fans and dehumidifiers, and remove soaked rugs and contents. Professional mitigation is often worth it for anything beyond a small area.</li>
<li><strong>Call a plumber</strong> and keep the invoice, which usually states where the break was and what caused it.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> with the date the pipe burst and when you discovered it.</li>
<li><strong>Keep every receipt</strong>, including mitigation, plumbing, and extra living expenses if the home is not livable.</li>
</ol>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/collapsed-ceiling-water-damage-adjuster.webp" alt="Adjuster with a clipboard standing in a living room beneath a collapsed, water-damaged ceiling" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A pipe that bursts in the attic can bring down drywall and soak insulation, flooring, and furniture below.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Documenting a freeze damage claim</h2>

<p>Water damage spreads in ways that are easy to miss. Wet insulation in the attic, moisture behind cabinets, swollen baseboards, and wicking in drywall may not be obvious the first day. A complete claim usually includes:</p>

<ul>
<li>Room-by-room photos and video, including ceilings, walls, flooring, cabinets, and contents.</li>
<li>Moisture readings and drying logs from the mitigation company.</li>
<li>A list of damaged personal property with age and approximate cost.</li>
<li>The plumber's invoice and the burst pipe section.</li>
<li>Notes on the heat, power, and occupancy at the time of the freeze.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lays this out step by step. When the insurer's estimate arrives, compare it line by line with what you documented. Freeze claims are often underpaid when the estimate covers only visibly wet drywall and leaves out insulation, cabinetry, matching flooring, or contents. Our guide to <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a> explains how depreciation may affect your first payment.</p>

<h2>Ice-damaged trees and roofs</h2>

<p>Ice storms like the one in 2023 cause a different kind of damage. Many policies list "weight of ice, snow, or sleet" as a covered cause of loss, and a limb that breaks under ice and falls through the roof is generally treated like any other falling object. Common issues include:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Limbs on the roof:</strong> punctured decking, broken shingles or metal panels, and damaged gutters.</li>
<li><strong>Gutters and eaves</strong> pulled loose by the weight of ice and icicles.</li>
<li><strong>Fences, carports, and outbuildings</strong> crushed by fallen trees, usually handled under "other structures" coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Tree removal</strong>, which many policies cover only in limited amounts and only when the tree damaged a covered structure.</li>
</ul>

<p>As with wind damage, photograph the tree where it landed before it is cut up, and tarp any openings right away. Our article on <a href="/wind-damage-insurance-claims-san-marcos-tx/">wind damage insurance claims</a> covers fallen-tree coverage in more detail.</p>

<h2>Preparing for the next hard freeze</h2>

<p>The best freeze claim is the one you never have to file, and preparation also strengthens your position if a pipe breaks anyway. Before a forecast hard freeze, keep an eye on the <a href="/weather/">current San Marcos weather</a> and work through a short list:</p>

<ul>
<li>Know where your main water shut-off valve is, and make sure it turns.</li>
<li>Insulate exposed pipes in attics, garages, and exterior walls, and cover outdoor hose bibs.</li>
<li>Disconnect garden hoses and drain irrigation backflow preventers if your system allows it.</li>
<li>Open cabinet doors under sinks on exterior walls and let faucets drip during extended cold.</li>
<li>If you will be away or a property is empty, keep the heat on at a reasonable setting or shut off and drain the water, and note what you did.</li>
<li>Photograph your thermostat setting and any steps you took. That record can matter if coverage questions come up later.</li>
</ul>

<p>Landlords should share these steps with tenants in writing. If water damage happens despite your efforts, our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page explains how those claims typically work.</p>

<h2>How a San Marcos public adjuster can help with freeze claims</h2>

